<!DOCTYPE node PUBLIC "-//freedesktop//DTD D-BUS Object Introspection 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/dbus/1.0/introspect.dtd">
<!-- Bus: org.templaros.Session. Path: /org/templaros/Session/Lifecycle. Ending, suspending or locking
     the session.

     Before this existed there was no way out of a TemplarOS session at all: the session ends when
     templar-comp.service stops (session/templar-session/src/main.rs), and nothing a user could reach
     stopped it. A tester had to switch to a virtual terminal and kill the compositor by hand.

     NOT polkit-gated, and deliberately not in templar-hostd. Every method here forwards to
     org.freedesktop.login1, which already authorises these actions for the active seat through
     its own polkit policy (org.freedesktop.login1.power-off and friends, allow_active=yes). Doing
     it again in TemplarOS would mean an administrator prompt to shut down your own laptop, and would
     put a session concern in the privileged daemon that ADR-0003 scopes to administrative
     actions like apt and deployments. sessiond runs unprivileged and needs no new privilege to
     make these calls.

     The Can* methods mirror logind's own so a surface can grey out what is genuinely
     unavailable rather than offering a control that fails on click. Each returns one of
     "yes", "no", "na", "challenge" - logind's own vocabulary, passed through unchanged rather
     than flattened to a boolean, because "challenge" (would need authentication) and "na" (not
     supported by this machine) are different facts and a UI may want to say so. -->
<node>
  <interface name="org.templaros.Session.Lifecycle1">
    <!-- End this session. Stops templaros-session.target through logind's TerminateSession. -->
    <method name="Logout"/>
    <method name="Reboot"/>
    <method name="PowerOff"/>
    <method name="Suspend"/>

    <!-- Lock the session.

         ADR-0010 makes the lock screen compositor-owned: templar-comp implements
         ext-session-lock-v1 in full (compositor/templar-comp/src/session_lock.rs), and only a
         Wayland client can drive it. sessiond is deliberately not that client — it speaks no
         Wayland at all — so this method does not lock the screen itself. It emits
         LockRequested, and templar-shell, which already holds the compositor's trusted-shell
         connection, is what calls lock() on the protocol.

         This is why Lock returns as soon as the request is out rather than once the screen is
         covered: the caller has asked for a lock, and the surface appears a frame or two later.
         A caller needing confirmed coverage watches Locked, emitted from NotifyLocked. -->
    <method name="Lock"/>

    <!-- Somebody asked for the session to be locked (Lock, or `templar session lock`).

         The trusted shell listens for this and drives ext-session-lock-v1. Additive in
         Lifecycle1 version 1; a client that does not know this signal is unaffected. -->
    <signal name="LockRequested"/>

    <!-- The session's own edges, so something can count session time rather than wall-clock time.

         A laptop asleep for a week aged nothing; a screen locked overnight aged nothing. Anything
         that ages with use - the Home garden above all - needs to know when this session was
         actually being used, and no existing signal says so. These four are that record.

         SessionStarted fires once, when templar-sessiond has claimed its bus name: a new session.
         Locked fires only after the trusted shell observes the compositor's protected-frame
         acknowledgment covering all active outputs. Unlocked fires from NotifyUnlocked.
         Sleeping carries true on the way
         down and false on the way back, mirroring logind's PrepareForSleep, which sessiond
         watches with one match rule and no polling.

         Additive in Lifecycle1 version 1; a client that does not know them is unaffected. -->
    <signal name="SessionStarted"/>
    <signal name="Locked"/>
    <signal name="Unlocked"/>
    <signal name="Sleeping">
      <arg name="going_to_sleep" type="b"/>
    </signal>

    <!-- The compositor turned the screen off for want of input (true) or back on at the next
         input (false), from NotifyIdle. An unlocked session nobody is touching is not being used,
         so session time stops here too. Additive in Lifecycle1 version 1. -->
    <signal name="Idle">
      <arg name="idle" type="b"/>
    </signal>

    <!-- The session was unlocked. Only the trusted shell may say so.

         ext-session-lock-v1's unlock_and_destroy is the only thing that clears the compositor's
         lock, and templar-shell is the only process that calls it (ADR-0010), so templar-shell is the
         only process that knows the lock is gone. It reports that here and this emits Unlocked.

         Restricted to templar-shell.service, identified by the caller's cgroup. That is a
         correctness guard rather than a security one - the session bus is already the trust
         boundary for Logout and PowerOff, which are unguarded - but a stray caller would corrupt
         the session-time record for everything downstream, and there is no reason to accept one. -->
    <method name="NotifyUnlocked"/>

    <!-- Only templar-shell.service may report the compositor's confirmed lock barrier. -->
    <method name="NotifyLocked"/>

    <!-- The screen went off for want of input (true), or came back on (false). Only the
         compositor may say so: templar-comp sees every input event and is the only honest idle
         source in the session (compositor/templar-comp/src/idle.rs). Restricted to
         templar-comp.service by the caller's cgroup, for the same correctness reason as
         NotifyUnlocked. -->
    <method name="NotifyIdle">
      <arg name="idle" type="b" direction="in"/>
    </method>

    <!-- "yes" | "no" | "na" | "challenge", straight from logind. -->
    <method name="CanReboot">
      <arg name="result" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <method name="CanPowerOff">
      <arg name="result" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <method name="CanSuspend">
      <arg name="result" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
  </interface>
</node>
